
  README FILE FOR INSTALLING AND EXECUTING MCNPX UNDER WINDOWS

The following information is provided to install MCNPX 
version 2.4.0 on a Windows 9X, NT, or 2000 platform. 
This is an F90 version of MCNPX which uses standard F90 allocation
schemes for dynamic variables. Over 200 test problems were run to
verify this version (170+ MCNP and 30+ MCNPX). Section 1.0 is for
those that have downloaded only the executables. Section 2.0 is
for those who have downloaded the source and want to build an MCNPX
executable. Section 3.0 discusses cross section issues.

1.0 USING THE PC WINDOWS EXECUTABLES ONLY

The following steps will install the MCNPX 2.4.0 executables for
Windows:

(1) Make a folder MCNPX on a drive with enough disk space (~50
    Mbytes). Unzip the MCNPX_2.4.0.tar.gz file in that folder.

(2) Set the needed environment variables. There are different
    ways to do this with Windows 2000, but the simplest is to
    run a supplied batch file (SETUPX.BAT) whenever a Command
    Prompt window is created. To do this, modify the Command
    Prompt properties by right-clicking on Start=>Programs=>
    Accessories=>Command Prompt. Under the Shortcut Tab, modify
    Target to match the following:

    %SystemRoot%\system32\cmd.exe /K C:\MCNPX\BIN\SETUPX

    Note this batch file assumes the MCNPX folder was put on
    the C: drive. If that is not the case, edit this file as
    needed. This sets the DATAPATH variable to C:\MCNPX\DATA.
    If you already have the MCNP cross-section files in another
    directory, copy all the files in C:\MCNPX\DATA to your
    other directory and use that directory for the DATAPATH
    variable. For other Windows versions, this SETUPX.BAT file
    can be run from a DOS window to set the environment
    variables. Some of these variables are used only for our
    classes. To run this batch file manually from a Command
    Prompt window enter:

    C:>C:\MCNPX\BIN\SETUPX

(3) If graphics are desired, download and install an X-Server.
    Several flavors can be found on the internet (e.g., X-Deep/32,
    WRQ Reflection X, Exceed, etc.). I suggest downloading a trial
    version to make sure it works right - both X-Deep/32 and
    Reflection X work for me. If you choose X-Deep/32, make it
    a bit more user friendly by doing the following. Start the 
    X-server from Start=>Programs=>X-Deep_32=>X-Server. Click 
    "Try", then "Select", and then "Normal Mode" (if connected
    to a LAN it may take a few seconds finding remote hosts after
    "Select" is clicked). Edit the properties by right-clicking
    on the title bar, then X-Server Menu=>X-Server Options=>Window
    Modes=>Multiple MS Window Mode. Then click Apply and Yes to
    restart. Be sure to start the X-Server before running MCNPX
    for plotting.

(4) If you use mesh plots and GRIDCONV to convert the mesh data,
    consider downloading and installing Tecplot. GRIDCONV has a
    Tecplot option and the graphics turn out quite nice. See URL
    http://www.tecplot.com for more details.

See User's Notes in Install.html for information on how to run 
test cases with the executables contained in the mcnpx distribution.


2.0 BUILDING MCNPX

If you wish to modify the source or recreate the executables,
you will need the Compaq Visual Fortran (CVF) compiler, version
6.1 or later, and the MSVC compiler, version 5.0 or later. Once
CVF and MSVC are installed, follow the following steps:

(1) Make a folder MCNPX on a drive with enough disk space (~200
    Mbytes). Unzip the MCNPX_2.4.0.TAR.GZ file in that folder. I
    suggest using WinZip 8.0 or later as this is a GZIP/TAR file.

(2) Open a "Command Prompt" window, make a build directory, and
    CD to that directory. Execute the configure batch file:

    MKDIR MCNPX_BLD
    CD MCNPX_BLD
    ..\MCNPX\configure

    You must supply the appropriate path to this batch file
    which is located in the folder created in step #1. At the
    end of this process, some files specific to a Windows
    build will be unzipped. Click on "UNZIP", then "OK", then
    "CLOSE" to complete the configure.

(3) Enter the following command in the "Command Prompt" window:

    SET PATH

    If your PATH environment variable is longer than 255
    characters, you will need to complete this step. If not,
    go to step #4. Due to a limitation of GNU Make on Windows,
    you may need to execute the MAKEPATH batch file. You will
    need to edit this file if you installed MCNPX in a folder
    other than C:\MCNPX or you installed CVF or MSVC in non-
    default directories. Go into the folder created in step #1
    and CD into BIN. Then edit MAKEPATH.BAT to change the last
    entry on the PATH environment variable to reflect the
    folder created in step #1. You may also need to edit the
    other paths on the PATH and LIB variables if you installed
    CVF or MSVC in nondefault folders. Now execute this batch
    file (simply type in "MAKEPATH") and return to the build
    directory. This should limit the number of characters to
    less than 255 for the PATH environment variable.

(4) Execute GNU Make by typing "MAKE" in the "Command Prompt"
    window. You can CD into any subdirectory and build any
    subcomponent of MCNPX just as on a Unix platform. If you
    wish to run the test suite, CD into SRC\TEST and execute
    "MAKE". The MAKE executable is provided in the MCNPX\BIN
    directory. The X11 library and include files are provided
    in MCNPX\LIB and should not be moved from here.

Patches to MCNPX can be developed, as done for MCNP; however one
should contact us for the needed script file and instructions to
apply such a patch. If a "stack overflow" error is generated, this
is NOT an MCNPX bug. A stack limit must be specified upon linking.
The included executable has a stack limit of 512 MBytes. This can
be increased by editing the Makfile.h file in the 
MCNPX_BLD\SRC\MCNPX directory (~line 66) and rebuilding MCNPX.

3.0 CROSS SECTION DATA

Cross section libraries are included in the DLC-200/MCNPXDATA 
(RSICC ID: D00205ALLCP03) distribution.  It includes the latest 
electron data file (EL03) which requires an MCNP 4C based code. 
Note that the xsdir file included in D00205ALLCP03 is complete;
therefore, xsdir does not need editing unless you are supplying
additional cross sections.  Expanding the DLC-205 cross sections 
will create a directory called "mcnpxdata" under your current 
directory which will contain the cross sections.


This version of MCNPX was compiled with XS64, or 64-bit cross sections, 
as is MCNP 4C/4C2 distributed from RSICC, thus you can use the Type 2
data distributed with MCNP 4C for MCNPX problems. You must be
certain to use those labeled "DVF", as the MCNPX executable with
this distribution was compiled with CVF 6.1 (formerly DVF). The
following steps should be followed to include the LA150 data with
the RSICC DVF Type 2 libraries:


The following instructions were written for beta testers:
(I) Convert the LA150 libraries to Type 2 files by running MAKXS
in the C:\MCNPX\DATA directory. This will create files XSDIR2,
TPRINT (MAKXS output file), LA150h2, LA150n2, and LA150u2.

(II) Copy the new directory lines from the file XSDIR2 (grab all
lines after the word "directory") and paste at the end of the XSDIR
file supplied with the RSICC distribution.

(III) Copy the RSICC Type 2 data files, including the modified
XSDIR file, into C:\MCNPX\DATA.

After completing these steps you should be able to run MCNPX with
the LA150 data and/or the standard MCNP 4C data.


4.0 CHANGES TO THE USER INTERFACE

The following changes have been made to the user interface of
previous MCNPX versions (2.1.X, 2.2.X, 2.3.X):

(A) PHYS:N card entries are now:

    EMAX = upper limit for neutron energy, MeV (def.=huge).

    EMCNF = energy boundary above which neutrons are treated
            with implicit capture and below which they are
            treated with analog capture (def.=0).

    IUNR = 0/1 = on/off unresolved resonance range probability
                 tables (def.=0).

    DNB = -1/0/>0 = on/off/bias delayed neutrons (def.=-1).

    ECUT = maximum energy for use of table-based data (def.=20 MeV).

    FISNU = -1/0/>0 = isotope dependent Guassian multiplicity nu
                      /integer sampled average nu (old method)/
                      FWHM of Guassian fission multiplicity nu
                      applied to all fissionable isotopes.

    RECOIL = 0/>0 = off/bias light-ion recoil particles (def.=0).

(B) If you run "mcnpx.exe" with the "ip" options for plotting,
    you will now get the Interactive Plotter. This will be 
    described in detail at the Salt Lake City ANS meeting. See
    step #2 below for installing an X-Server.

(C) If you usually use PAW to visualize the mesh data, we have
    not been able to get this to work as of yet on the Windows
    platform. Consider using Tecplot as described under step #4
    below.

Call or email if any problems are encountered or if questions arise.
If you work at LANL, we can help you in getting the MCNP 4C cross-
section files.

